<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">ECOMOG</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1990">1990</a>–<a href="/years/1999">1999</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Economic Community of West African States Monitoring Group</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">ECOMOG, the Economic Community of West African States Monitoring Group, was a multinational West African armed force led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>. Formed in 1990 to intervene in the Liberian civil war, it held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monrovia">Monrovia</a> and later supervised the 1997 elections; it also intervened in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea-bissau">Guinea-Bissau</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ecomog.jpg" alt="ECOMOG" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carport · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ECOWAS_members.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The members of the Economic Community of West African States (ECOWAS) agreed a Protocol on Mutual Defence Assistance at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freetown">Freetown</a> on 29 May 1981, which provided for an allied armed force when needed. ECOMOG itself was created by the Standing Mediation Committee at its meeting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banjul">Banjul</a> on 6 and 7 August 1990. The Nigerian scholar Adekeye Adebajo has written that this body stood on shaky legal foundations, that the guidelines of the defence protocol were not followed, and that the force was justified largely on humanitarian grounds.</p><p>It was the English-speaking members of ECOWAS that acted, intervening in the Liberian civil war that had begun in 1989. Several French-speaking members strongly opposed the deployment, and the leaders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burkina-faso">Burkina Faso</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-coast">Côte d'Ivoire</a> supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-taylor-liberia">Charles Taylor</a> in his attempt to depose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-doe">Samuel Doe</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Unlike the usual <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> mission of the time, the force had to fight its way into a many-sided civil war to hold the factions apart. Most of its men and resources came from the Nigerian Armed Forces, with smaller units from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-gambia">The Gambia</a> and others. Its first commander was the Ghanaian Lieutenant General Arnold Quainoo; after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-doe">Samuel Doe</a> was killed by Prince Johnson's Independent National Patriotic Front of Liberia on 9 September 1990, Major General Joshua Dogonyaro took over, the first of an unbroken line of Nigerian commanders.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> sent 1,500 soldiers with some financial support from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, but withdrew them all by mid-January 1993, after six were killed in a confrontation with Taylor's supporters at Vahun on 28 May 1992. Corruption and organised looting by its troops led some Liberians to read the acronym as Every Car or Movable Object Gone. After Taylor was elected president on 19 July 1997, the last field commander, General Timothy Shelpidi, withdrew the force fully by the end of 1998.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> the force was the first credible attempt at a regional security initiative since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/organisation-of-african-unity">Organisation of African Unity</a> tried to send an Inter-African Force to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a> in 1981. A deployment of 1,700 men planned in 2001 for the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a> and Liberia never took place, for lack of funds and because of fighting in Liberia. In 2003, under pressure from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, ECOWAS launched a similar mission, ECOMIL, to halt the rebel occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monrovia">Monrovia</a> during the Second Liberian Civil War; it was soon succeeded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> mission <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations-mission-in-liberia">UNMIL</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Economic Community of West African States Monitoring Group” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A West African intervention force, <b>ECOMOG</b>, led by Nigeria, entered Monrovia in 1990.</blockquote><a href="/empire/liberia">Liberia · Military</a></li></ul></section>
